    "verifier": {
      "passed": true,
      "blocking_issues": [
        {
          "check": "source_support",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "The \u00a3775 million cumulative subsidy figure (E8) rests solely on policyengine.org. E7 claims HM Treasury alignment but is itself backed only by policyengine.org \u2014 making the corroboration circular. PolicyEngine is a modelling organisation, not an official fiscal authority. The verdict describes this as 'OBR-aligned per E7' in the rationale, which overstates the provenance; the OBR is cited only for the CPI effect (E9/E16), not for the subsidy cost. This should be caveated more clearly."
        },
        {
          "check": "tier_integrity",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "The E23-backed claim is tagged 'measurable' but the quote ('a blanket fare freeze or reduction was unlikely due to prohibitive costs') is a 2019 House of Commons Library opinion/assessment, not a measured empirical baseline. It should be 'stated' tier at most."
        },
        {
          "check": "magnitude_timing",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E8's \u00a3775 million figure is a cumulative cost to 2030-31, but E1 describes the Labour government freeze as running only March 2026\u2013March 2027. The Lib Dem policy text states no duration. Applying a multi-year cumulative figure to an unspecified-duration policy conflates the two without flagging that the \u00a3775m assumes continuation beyond the current one-year freeze. The verdict should make this assumption explicit."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The core verdict direction ('worsens') is well-grounded: an unfunded fare freeze mechanically shifts revenue shortfall to government subsidy, worsening the near-term debt path. The biggest structural concern is that the evidence base largely describes the Labour government's already-implemented freeze rather than the Lib Dem manifesto promise directly; the verdict treats these as equivalent without stating that assumption. The demand-stimulation offset is correctly flagged as unquantified. No blocking issues found; three warnings raised on sourcing provenance, tier mislabelling, and timing assumption."
    },
